# 9. Redis Memory Baseline

The following Redis memory information was captured:

```text
used_memory:             984008 bytes
used_memory_human:       960.95K

used_memory_rss:         16084992 bytes
used_memory_rss_human:   15.34M

used_memory_peak:        1110720 bytes
used_memory_peak_human:  1.06M

used_memory_dataset:     243288 bytes
```

Redis configuration:

```text
maxmemory: 0
maxmemory_policy: noeviction
```

## Interpretation

`maxmemory: 0` means that Redis has no explicit memory limit configured.

The eviction policy is:

```text
noeviction
```

Therefore, this baseline does not represent a memory-constrained Redis deployment.

---

# 10. Memory Fragmentation

Redis reported:

```text
mem_fragmentation_ratio: 16.65
```

This value should **not** be interpreted as a serious fragmentation problem for this experiment.

The Redis dataset was extremely small:

```text
Dataset ≈ 243 KB
```

while process RSS was approximately:

```text
15.34 MB
```

At very small memory allocations, fixed process and allocator overhead can make the fragmentation ratio appear disproportionately large.

This metric will be revisited when the experimental dataset becomes significantly larger.

